; ============================================================
; TITLE:    A Tree and Its Stop Rule
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     recursion, trees, fractal, stop rule, state
; SUMMARY:  Grows a branching tree where every branch is a smaller tree, with leaves at the tips and a careful explanation of why the turtle must come home.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   A tree is a trunk with two smaller trees growing out of its top.
;   Each of those is a trunk with two smaller trees, and so on. TREE
;   draws that idea literally. Without a stop rule it would go on
;   forever, drawing branches too tiny to see. The stop rule here says:
;   when a branch would be shorter than 6 steps, draw a leaf instead.
;   The branches get thinner and greener as they get smaller.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the stop rule line,
;        if :size < 6 [leaf stop]
;      right-click the red dot, choose "Stop only when..." and type
;        :size < 6
;      Run. The turtle is at the tip of a twig. Open the Call Stack and
;      count the TREEs: that is how many branches lie between the trunk
;      and this leaf.
;   3. Remove it and put a breakpoint on the LAST line of TREE,
;        back :size
;      Turn on the condition  :size > 60  so it only stops for big
;      branches, and watch the turtle return down the trunk.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * the stop rule (base case) and what happens without one
;   * the state-transparent rule: a recursive drawing must put the turtle
;     back exactly where and how it found it (position, heading, pen)
;   * using inputs to control color and thickness
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Change the stop rule to :size < 20 and then to :size < 2.
;   * Make the angle different on each side (20 left, 35 right).
;   * Use (random 15) + 20 as the angle for a wilder tree.
;   * Delete the  back :size  line. Why does everything break?
; ============================================================

to tree :size
  ; THE STOP RULE: too small to be a branch, so be a leaf and stop.
  if :size < 6 [leaf stop]
  branch.pen :size
  fd :size
  ; Left subtree. 30 degrees left, 75 percent the size.
  lt 30
  tree :size * 0.75
  ; Right subtree. Turn 60 so we end up 30 to the right of straight.
  rt 60
  tree :size * 0.68
  ; Face straight up again, then walk back down this branch. Now the
  ; turtle is exactly where our caller left it, facing the same way.
  lt 30
  ; The subtrees changed the pen (the leaves are thin and green), so
  ; set it back before retracing this branch. That is part of putting
  ; things back the way we found them.
  branch.pen :size
  back :size
end

; :t is 1 for the trunk and near 0 for twigs, so the color slides from
; bark brown down to fresh green, and the pen gets thinner.
to branch.pen :size
  localmake "t :size / 110
  setpensize :size / 7
  setpencolor (list 60 + 50 * :t 160 - 110 * :t 40 - 20 * :t)
end

; A little green leaf: two arcs back to back. It also leaves the turtle
; as it found it, which is why it fits into the stop rule so easily.
to leaf
  setpensize 1
  setpencolor (list 40 + random 80 150 + random 80 40)
  filled pencolor [rt 25 repeat 10 [fd 1.2 lt 5] lt 130 repeat 10 [fd 1.2 lt 5] lt 155]
end

to ground
  setpencolor [90 60 30]
  penup setxy -400 -260 setheading 90 pendown
  filled [110 80 40] [fd 800 rt 90 fd 40 rt 90 fd 800 rt 90 fd 40 rt 90]
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [200 230 255]
ground
penup setxy 0 -260 setheading 0 pendown
tree 110
